m A which have no corresponding rows in B, effectively preserving the LEFT JOIN semantics.\nCase 3:\n**Conditions**: This applies when a subsequent WHERE clause filter mandates the presence of non-NULL values in columns from the non-preserving side of an OUTER JOIN. This specific filtering effectively mandates that there's a matching row in the non-preserving side's table, thus allowing the OUTER JOIN to be converted to an INNER JOIN for efficiency.\n**Transformations**: Convert OUTER JOINS to INNER JOINS when followed by WHERE clauses that eliminate the possibility of NULL results from the non-preserving side. For example, `SELECT * FROM A LEFT OUTER JOIN B ON condition1 WHERE B.column IS NOT NULL` can be efficiently rewritten as `SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON condition1` because the WHERE clause enforces a logic that requires a match in B for every row returned.\n```\n\nLogical Plan Changes After Rewrite: ```\n  LogicalSort(sort0=[$0(c_customer_id)], dir0=[ASC], fetch=[100])\n    LogicalProject(c_customer_id=[$34(c_customer_id)])\n      LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(>($3(sr_reversed_charge), $SCALAR_QUERY({\n  LogicalProject(EXPR$0=[*($0(sr_reversed_charge), 1.2:DECIMAL(2, 1))])\n    LogicalAggregate(group=[{}], agg#0=[AVG($0)])\n      LogicalProject(ctr_total_return=[$3(sr_reversed_charge)])\n        LogicalFilter(condition=[=($cor0.ctr_store_sk, $1(sr_store_sk))])\n          LogicalAggregate(group=[{0, 1, 2}], ctr_total_return=[SUM($3)])\n            LogicalProject(ctr_customer_sk=[$3(sr_customer_sk)], ctr_store_sk=[$7(sr_store_sk)], ctr_reason_sk=[$8(sr_reason_sk)], sr_reversed_charge=[$17(sr_reversed_charge)])\n              LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($0(sr_returned_date_sk), $20(d_date_sk)), =($26(d_year), 2002), >=(/($11(sr_return_amt), $10(sr_return_quantity)), 149), <=(/($11(sr_return_amt), $10(sr_return_quantity)), 208))])\n                LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                  LogicalTableScan(table=[[store_returns]])\n  